'''Robert Bruce Charles'''<ref name="maine-gop-nominates">{{cite web | url=https://www.themainewire.com/2024/11/maine-gop-nominates-robert-b-charles-for-state-attorney-general | title=Maine GOP Nominates Robert B. Charles for State Attorney General | date=26 November 2024 }}</ref> (born  August 23, 1960)<ref>{{cite web|url=https://history.state.gov/departmenthistory/people/charles-robert-b|title=Robert B. Charles (1960–)|work=Department of State|access-date=September 3, 2022}}</ref> is an American [[lawyer]]. He served as the Assistant Secretary of State for International Narcotics and Law Enforcement Affairs from 2003 to 2005. He is the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]] nominee for [[governor of Maine]] in the [[2026 Maine gubernatorial election|2026 election]].

Charles founded The Charles Group, a consulting firm based in [[Washington, D.C.]]<ref name=":0">"The Charles Group, LLC." http://charlesgroupdc.com/default.aspx {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130909171609/http://charlesgroupdc.com/default.aspx |date=2013-09-09 }}</ref>

==Early life and education==
Charles grew up in a small town of [[Wayne, Maine]],<ref name="maine-gop-nominates"/> where his mother, Doris Anne, was a teacher.<ref>{{cite web |title=Meet Bobby |url=https://www.bobbyformaine.com/meet-charles |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251207151818/https://www.bobbyformaine.com/meet-charles |archive-date=December 7, 2025 |access-date=September 29, 2026}}</ref> He married Marina Timasheff in 1988.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.nytimes.com/1988/10/17/style/marina-timasheff-and-robert-b-charles-are-wed.html | title=Marina Timasheff and Robert B. Charles Are Wed | work=The New York Times | date=17 October 1988 }}</ref>

He received a [[Juris Doctor]] from [[Columbia Law School]] in 1987, a [[Master of Arts]] in philosophy, politics, and economics (PPE) on a Keasbey Scholarship from [[New College, Oxford]] in 1984, and a [[Bachelor of Arts]] from [[Dartmouth College]] in 1982.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://2001-2009.state.gov/outofdate/bios/c/25156.htm | title=Charles, Robert B }}</ref>

==Political career ==
Between 2003 and 2005, Charles worked for International Narcotics and Law Enforcement Affairs (INL).<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://history.state.gov/departmenthistory/people/charles-robert-b|title=Robert B. Charles – People – Department History – Office of the Historian|access-date=2017-09-21|archive-date=2017-09-22|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170922051849/https://history.state.gov/departmenthistory/people/charles-robert-b|url-status=live}}</ref> One of the INL's missions during this time was to conduct counter-narcotics and build police forces in Afghanistan.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://2001-2009.state.gov/outofdate/bios/c/25156.htm|title=U.S. Policy Towards Narcoterrorism in Afghanistan – US Department of State Archive|access-date=2026-08-28|url-status=live}}</ref> More than a dozen federal audits conducted over this period found a "failure to accomplish primary foreign policy goals", and criticized the operation's leaders for failing to effectively countering narcotics, despite receiving 1.2 billion dollars in funding.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://themainemonitor.org/bobby-charles-foreign-policy-record/|title=Bobby Charles ran a bureau that spent billions in Afghanistan and Iraq. Watchdogs say it failed – The Maine Monitor|access-date=2026-08-28|url-status=|quote=More than a dozen federal audits criticized the bureau Charles led for roughly 18 months between 2003 and 2005. Auditors found poor management, billions spent without accountability, and failure to accomplish primary foreign policy goals. Leaders of the bureau were faulted for their work on the agency’s key goals of training national police and countering narcotics.}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Department Of State. The Office of Electronic Information |first=Bureau of Public Affairs |title=2005 International Narcotics Control Strategy Report |url=https://2001-2009.state.gov/r/pa/prs/ps/2005/43018.htm |access-date=2026-09-01 |website=state.gov |publisher=Department Of State. The Office of Electronic Information, Bureau of Public Affairs. |language=en |quote="To stop drugs from flourishing, the President committed more than $1.2 billion dollars in funding for our efforts" [...] [Questioner:] "Is there any way to describe what's happened in Afghanistan as anything other than a abysmal failure of U.S. counternarcotics efforts there? [... This] report says that Afghanistan essentially amounts for the world's heroin production [... and according to the] report [...] 40 to 60 percent of the GDP is illicit drugs, which -- and you say that it's on the verge of becoming a narco-state."}}</ref>

== 2026 gubernatorial campaign ==
{{Main|2026 Maine gubernatorial election}}
In early April 2025, Charles announced his run in the [[2026 Maine gubernatorial election]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kobin |first=Billy |date=2025-04-15 |title=He served under George W. Bush. Now he’s running for Maine governor. |url=https://www.bangordailynews.com/2025/04/15/politics/elections/maine-election-2026-republican-governor-candidate-bobby-charles/ |access-date=2025-09-01 |website=[[Bangor Daily News]] |language=en-US}}</ref> On June 9, he won the Republican nomination by a wide margin.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kail |first=Benjamin |url=https://www.bangordailynews.com/2026/06/09/politics/bobby-charles-leads-republican-gubernatorial-primary-going-into-ranked-choice/ |access-date=2026-09-01 |title=Bobby Charles has big lead in Republican gubernatorial primary going into ranked-choice count|website=Bangor Daily News |language=en}}</ref> He will face Democratic nominee [[Hannah Pingree]] and Independent [[Rick Bennett (Maine politician)|Rick Bennett]] in the November general election.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Rogers |first=Brad |date=2026-06-19 |title=Maine gubernatorial candidates trade barbs on first day of general campaign |url=https://wgme.com/news/local/maine-gubernatorial-candidates-trade-barbs-on-first-day-of-general-campaign-democrat-hannah-pingree-republican-bobby-charles-independent-rick-bennett |access-date=2026-06-20 |website=WGME |language=en}}</ref>

Charles is active on social media during the campaign, having uploaded numerous videos about himself conversing with local residents in Maine.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Bobby Charles for Maine Governor |url=https://www.bobbyformaine.com/ |access-date=2026-09-01 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-08-26 |title=Are Maine political campaigns required to disclose when they use AI in ads? |url=https://wgme.com/news/i-team/are-maine-political-campaigns-required-to-disclose-when-they-use-ai-in-ads |access-date=2026-08-28 |website=WGME News |language=en}}</ref> He also frequently uses [[generative AI]] on social media.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Romans |first=Payton |date=2026-08-18 |title=Are Maine political campaigns required to disclose when they use AI in ads? |url=https://wgme.com/news/i-team/are-maine-political-campaigns-required-to-disclose-when-they-use-ai-in-ads |access-date=2026-10-27 |website=WGME |language=en}}</ref>

In September 2026, Charles's sister Anita announced that she would not be supporting her brother in an [[op-ed]] in the ''[[Portland Press Herald]]''.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-09-09 |title=Bobby Charles’ sister says she will vote for Hannah Pingree for Maine governor |url=https://www.pressherald.com/2026/09/09/bobby-charles-sister-says-she-will-vote-for-hannah-pingree-for-maine-governor/ |access-date=2026-09-09 |website=The Portland Press Herald |language=en-US}}</ref> Anita criticized her brother for his conduct during the campaign, for his views on abortion, and for maintaining a friendship with the late [[Dennis Hastert]], the former [[Speaker of the United States House of Representatives]], after allegations of sexual misconduct came to light in 2015.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-09-09 |title=I'm Bobby Charles' sister. I'm voting for Hannah Pingree. |url=https://www.pressherald.com/2026/09/09/im-bobby-charles-sister-im-voting-for-hannah-pingree-opinion/ |access-date=2026-09-09 |website=The Portland Press Herald |language=en-US}}</ref>

In October 2026, the Charles campaign was fined over $22,000 by the Maine Ethics Commission for campaign donation violations.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-10-26 |title=Bobby Charles fined more than $22,000 for Maine gubernatorial campaign donation violations |url=https://www.pressherald.com/2026/10/26/bobby-charles-fined-more-than-22000-for-maine-gubernatorial-campaign-donation-violations/ |access-date=2026-10-27 |website=The Portland Press Herald |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== Immigration ===
Charles is a supporter of enforcing immigration law via [[Immigration and Customs Enforcement]] (ICE) presence in Maine.<ref>{{Cite web |last=jennab |date=2026-02-27 |title=RACE TO THE RIGHT: Bobby Charles Doubles Down on Supporting Trump Administration’s ICE Operations as Mainers Demand Accountability - Maine Democratic Party |url=https://mainedems.org/race-to-the-right-bobby-charles-doubles-down-on-supporting-trump-administrations-ice-operations-as-mainers-demand-accountability/ |access-date=2026-09-17 |website=mainedems.org |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Maine |first=Susan Cover |title=GOP candidate Charles to work with Trump on fraud allegations |url=https://spectrumlocalnews.com/me/maine/politics/2026/06/03/gop-candidate-charles-to-work-with-trump-on-fraud-allegations |access-date=2026-09-17 |website=spectrumlocalnews.com |language=en}}</ref> In August 2026, a statement from his campaign stated “everybody who is legally entitled to vote should be free from any intimidation and should be able to vote", and that "anybody who is not legally entitled to vote should not be voting.” He has also floated the idea of asking [[Tom Homan]] to have ICE and other law enforcement present at polling places.<ref name="audio-ice-polls">{{Cite web |date=2026-08-26 |title=Republican nominee for Maine governor says he wants ICE agents at polling places in the state |url=https://apnews.com/article/bobby-charles-audio-ice-polls-maine-governor-4e77e0f06a28d2a8f42b51ce28a91677 |access-date=2026-08-28 |website=AP News |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://wgme.com/news/local/gubernatorial-candidate-bobby-charles-reportedly-wants-ice-agents-at-maine-polls-republicans-democrates-trump-administration-governor-voting|title=Gubernatorial candidate Bobby Charles reportedly wants ICE agents at Maine polls|date=August 2026|website=WGME News}}</ref>

